We can use this proportion:

$\begin{array}{rcl}
&\triangle JKL
\\
{\text{opposite to } \atop 40\degree\text{ angle}}
&\overbrace{\underbrace{\!\!\!\!\!\left\{ 
\maroonD{\dfrac{KL}{6.4}}=\blueE{\dfrac{JL}{7.7}}
\right\}\!\!\!\!\!}}
&{\text{adjacent to } \atop 40\degree\text{ angle}}
\\
&\text{triangle }\boxed{3}
\end{array}$



[[☃ image 1]]

Now we can rewrite the equation to show the ratios of the side lengths *within* each triangle.

$\begin{array}{rcl}
&{\text{opposite to } \atop 40\degree\text{ angle}}
\\
\scriptsize\triangle JKL
&\overbrace{\underbrace{\!\!\!\!\!\left\{ 
\dfrac{KL}{JL}=\dfrac{6.4}{7.7}
\right\}\!\!\!\!\!}}
&\scriptsize\text{triangle }\boxed{3}
\\
&{\text{adjacent to } \atop 40\degree\text{ angle}}
\end{array}$
